INSERT INTO `terms` (`term_id`, `term_name`, `term_category`, `posted_by`, `reviewed_by`, `term_desc`, `added_date`, `updated_date`) VALUES
(1, 'wrongful death attorney', 'w', 'Jahan Rani', 'Aman Singh', '<h2>Wrongful Death Attorney: Meaning, Types, Solution, Examples - Imperialpedia</h2>\r\n\r\n<p>A wrongful death attorney is a legal professional who specializes in representing families and other beneficiaries of a person who has died due to the negligence, recklessness, or intentional actions of another individual or entity. These attorneys have the necessary expertise and experience to navigate the complex legal process involved in filing a wrongful death lawsuit and seeking compensation for the losses suffered by the deceased person&#39;s family or estate. They are responsible for gathering evidence, negotiating settlements, representing the plaintiff in court, and determining the types of damages that may be recoverable. A wrongful death attorney also helps families understand their legal rights and provides guidance and support throughout the legal process.</p>\r\n', '2023-03-25 12:19:12', '2023-03-27 17:04:08'),
(2, 'wrongful death lawsuit', 'w', 'Jahan Rani', 'Aman Singh', '<h2>Wrongful Death Lawsuit: Meaning, Types, Solution, Examples - Imperialpedia</h2>\r\n\r\n<p>A wrongful death lawsuit is a legal action taken by the surviving family members, dependents, or beneficiaries of a person who has died due to the fault of another person, entity or corporation. The purpose of the lawsuit is to seek compensation for the damages incurred as a result of the death, such as medical expenses, funeral costs, lost income, and pain and suffering. The lawsuit is typically filed against the party or parties responsible for the death, and can be brought in civil court. In a wrongful death lawsuit, the plaintiff must prove that the defendant was at fault for the death, either through negligence or intentional wrongdoing. The damages that may be recovered in a wrongful death lawsuit vary depending on the circumstances of the case and the laws of the jurisdiction in which the case is filed</p>\r\n', '2023-03-25 12:27:32', '2023-03-27 17:17:24'),
(3, 'a b trust', 'A ', 'Jahan Rani', 'Aman Singh', '<h2><big><strong>A-B Trust: Definition, How It Works, Tax Benefits</strong></big></h2>\r\n\r\n<p><big><strong>What Is an A-B Trust?</strong></big></p>\r\n\r\n<p>An AB trust is a type of estate planning trust that can help married couples minimize estate taxes and protect their assets. It is created by dividing a couple&#39;s assets into two separate trusts upon the death of one spouse, with the goal of maximizing the use of each spouse&#39;s individual estate tax exemption.</p>\r\n\r\n<p><strong><big>What Is A Trust For Couples?</big></strong></p>\r\n\r\n<p>The &quot;A&quot; trust, also known as the surviving spouse&#39;s trust, is funded with the assets of the first spouse to die and is designed to provide income and principal to the surviving spouse. The assets in the &quot;A&quot; trust are included in the surviving spouse&#39;s estate for tax purposes.</p>\r\n\r\n<p><strong><big>What Is B Trust For Couples?</big></strong></p>\r\n\r\n<p>The &quot;B&quot; trust, also known as the bypass trust or the credit shelter trust, is funded with the assets of the first spouse to die up to the amount of the estate tax exemption. This trust is designed to provide income and principal to the surviving spouse and other beneficiaries, but the assets in the &quot;B&quot; trust are not included in the surviving spouse&#39;s estate for tax purposes. This means that the assets in the &quot;B&quot; trust can pass to the beneficiaries without being subject to estate taxes.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>AB trusts can be a useful tool for married couples who have significant assets and want to minimise the impact of estate taxes. However, they may not be necessary or appropriate for every couple, and it is important to consult with an estate planning attorney or financial advisor to determine the best approach for your individual circumstances.</p>\r\n\r\n<p><big><strong>KEY GISTS</strong></big></p>\r\n\r\n<ol>\r\n	<li>When the first spouse dies, their share of the assets is transferred into Trust A. This trust is often referred to as the &quot;marital trust&quot; or &quot;QTIP trust.&quot; The surviving spouse is the beneficiary of this trust and can receive income and principal from it.</li>\r\n	<li>The assets in Trust A are not included in the first spouse&#39;s estate for estate tax purposes. Instead, they are included in the surviving spouse&#39;s estate.</li>\r\n	<li>When the surviving spouse dies, their assets are transferred into Trust B. This trust is often referred to as the &quot;bypass trust&quot; or &quot;credit shelter trust.&quot; The beneficiaries of this trust are typically the couple&#39;s children or other designated heirs.</li>\r\n	<li>The assets in Trust B are not included in the surviving spouse&#39;s estate for estate tax purposes. This means that they are not subject to estate tax when the surviving spouse dies.</li>\r\n</ol>\r\n\r\n<p>The main tax benefit of an A-B trust is that it allows couples to take advantage of both spouses&#39; estate tax exemptions. Each individual is allowed to pass a certain amount of assets to their heirs tax-free (the exemption amount), which is currently set at $11.7 million per person as of 2021. By dividing their assets into two separate trusts, a married couple can effectively double their estate tax exemption.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>Another benefit of an A-B trust is that it can help protect assets from creditors and ensure that they are distributed according to the couple&#39;s wishes. However, setting up and maintaining an A-B trust can be complex and expensive, so it&#39;s important to consult with an experienced estate planning attorney to determine if it&#39;s the right option for you.</p>\r\n\r\n<h2><big>Understanding an A-B Trust</big></h2>\r\n\r\n<p>An A-B Trust is a type of trust commonly used in estate planning to help married couples minimize <strong>estate taxes</strong> and efficiently transfer assets to their beneficiaries.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>The A-B Trust, also known as a &quot;credit shelter trust,&quot; is typically created by the couple&#39;s <strong>estate planning attorney</strong> and is based on the terms of their will or revocable living trust.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>Here&#39;s how it works:</p>\r\n\r\n<p>When the first spouse dies, their assets are divided into two trusts: the &quot;A&quot; trust and the &quot;B&quot; trust. The &quot;A&quot; trust, also known as the &quot;marital trust&quot; or &quot;QTIP trust,&quot; is designed to benefit the surviving spouse. The surviving spouse has access to the income and principal of the A trust during their lifetime.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>The &quot;B&quot; trust, also known as the &quot;<strong>bypass trust</strong>&quot; or &quot;credit shelter trust,&quot; is designed to benefit the couple&#39;s heirs. The assets in the B trust are not subject to estate tax when the surviving spouse dies, so they can pass to the heirs without being subject to further taxation.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>By using an A-B Trust, the couple can take advantage of both spouses&#39; estate tax exemptions and minimize the amount of estate taxes that must be paid. The A-B Trust is a complex estate planning tool that should only be created by an experienced estate planning attorney who understands the nuances of estate tax law.</p>\r\n\r\n<p><strong><big>Benefits of an A-B Trust</big></strong><br />\r\n<big>There are several benefits of an A-B Trust in estate planning, including:</big></p>\r\n\r\n<ol>\r\n	<li><strong>Reducing estate taxes</strong>: The primary benefit of an A-B Trust is that it can help reduce the amount of estate taxes that must be paid when both spouses pass away. By splitting assets between the A and B trusts, the couple can take advantage of both spouses&#39; estate tax exemptions, potentially saving thousands or even millions of dollars in taxes.</li>\r\n	<li><strong>Protecting assets</strong>: The B trust can protect the couple&#39;s assets from creditors, lawsuits, and other potential threats. Since the assets in the B trust are held separately from the surviving spouse&#39;s assets, they can be shielded from any legal issues that may arise.</li>\r\n	<li><strong>Providing for the surviving spouse</strong>: The A trust provides for the surviving spouse&#39;s needs by giving them access to income and principal during their lifetime. This can help ensure that the surviving spouse is taken care of financially, even if the other spouse has passed away.</li>\r\n	<li><strong>Preserving wealth for future generations</strong>: The B trust can preserve the couple&#39;s wealth for future generations by passing assets to their heirs without being subject to further estate taxes. This can help ensure that the couple&#39;s children and grandchildren receive as much of their wealth as possible.</li>\r\n	<li><strong>Flexibility</strong>: An A-B Trust can be tailored to the couple&#39;s specific needs and circumstances, providing flexibility in estate planning. This allows the couple to create a plan that meets their unique goals and objectives, rather than being limited to a one-size-fits-all approach.</li>\r\n</ol>\r\n\r\n<h2><strong><big>Net Worth and A-B Trusts</big></strong></h2>\r\n\r\n<p>A-B Trusts can be particularly useful for married couples with a higher net worth because they can help minimize estate taxes and protect their assets for future generations.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>Net worth is the total value of a person&#39;s assets, including cash, investments, real estate, and other property, minus any liabilities or debts. When a married couple has a high net worth, they may be subject to estate taxes when they pass away, which can significantly reduce the amount of wealth that can be passed on to their heirs.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>By using an A-B Trust, the couple can take advantage of both spouses&#39; estate tax exemptions, potentially doubling the amount of assets that can be passed on tax-free. This can help preserve the couple&#39;s wealth for future generations and minimize the amount of taxes that must be paid.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>It&#39;s important to note that the exact amount of wealth a couple needs to have before an A-B Trust becomes necessary will vary based on state and federal laws, as well as the couple&#39;s individual circumstances. An experienced estate planning attorney can help determine whether an A-B Trust is right for a particular couple based on their net worth and other factors.</p>\r\n\r\n<h2><strong><big>Why Is an A-B Trust Obsolete?</big></strong></h2>\r\n\r\n<p>An A-B Trust was a popular estate planning tool in the past when the estate tax exemption was much lower than it is today. Prior to the Tax Cuts and Jobs Act of 2017, the federal estate tax exemption was set at $5.49 million per person, meaning that only estates valued at more than $5.49 million were subject to estate taxes.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>The Tax Cuts and Jobs Act increased the federal estate tax exemption significantly, and it is now set at $11.7 million per person (as of 2021). This means that fewer estates are subject to estate taxes, and many married couples may not need an A-B Trust to minimize their tax liability.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>In addition to the higher exemption, the Tax Cuts and Jobs Act also introduced a &quot;portability&quot; provision, which allows a surviving spouse to use any unused portion of their deceased spouse&#39;s estate tax exemption. This means that even if one spouse passes away and does not use their full exemption, the surviving spouse can still benefit from it without the need for an A-B Trust.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>However, there are still some situations where an A-B Trust may be beneficial, such as in states with lower estate tax exemptions or for couples with significant assets who want to take advantage of other benefits of an A-B Trust, such as asset protection or control over the distribution of their assets.&nbsp;</p>\r\n\r\n<p>Ultimately, whether an A-B Trust is necessary or not will depend on each couple&#39;s individual circumstances and goals. An experienced estate planning attorney can help determine the best approach for minimizing estate taxes and protecting assets based on a couple&#39;s net worth and other factors.</p>\r\n\r\n<h2><strong><big>What Is Another Name for an A-B Trust?</big></strong></h2>\r\n\r\n<p>An A-B Trust is also commonly known as a &quot;credit shelter trust.&quot; Other names for an A-B Trust include &quot;marital bypass trust,&quot; &quot;QTIP trust,&quot; &quot;family trust,&quot; and &quot;spousal lifetime access trust (SLAT).&quot; The specific name used for the trust may vary based on the terms of the trust document and the individual circumstances of the couple creating the trust.</p>\r\n', '2023-03-30 16:53:08', '2023-03-30 16:56:52');

